              "textField": "Medications in Australia are evaluated and regulated by the Therapeutic Goods Administration. If that regulation body deem the medication to be potentially abused or dangerous, they will be likely to designate it as a *Schedule 8* drug. Medications like opioids and two benzodiazepines- alprazolam (Xanax) and flunitrazepam are in this class. A medication that needs a doctor’s prescription is listed as a *Schedule 4*. If a medication simply needs to have someone around in order to give assistance and information if necessary is listed as a *Schedule 2* drug and only sold in pharmacies. But if you need to obtain it from a pharmacist, without a prescription, it is considered a *Schedule 3*, pharmacist only medication. Drugs like heroin that are illegal are considered *Schedule 9* drugs."
